    "narrative": "# Robin's 2025 Outside Memory\n\n## Welcome Message\n\nWelcome, Robin! We're thrilled to celebrate your year with Outside Online and our community of passionate adventurers. Over the past 366 days, you've been an exceptional member\u2014engaging with our content nearly 60 times a day and exploring everything from professional cycling to mountain trails. Your curiosity and dedication make you exactly the kind of reader we love to have in our community.\n\n---\n\n## Robin's 2025 Outside Memory\n\nYour year with us tells the story of someone deeply invested in endurance sports, the outdoors, and the intersection of adventure and science. Starting quietly in January with just 152 events, your engagement gradually built through the spring months before exploding in June\u2014the beginning of what would become your most active season. June marked a turning point with 2,293 events, and by August, you reached peak engagement with 4,574 events. This summer surge coincided perfectly with cycling season: the Tour de France dominated your reading in June and July, and you were consuming everything from race analysis to team dynamics.\n\nYour passion for professional cycling became increasingly evident as the year progressed. You followed the careers of Tadej Poga\u010dar (178 tagged articles), Jonas Vingegaard (143 tagged articles), and Remco Evenepoel (54 tagged articles) with the dedication of a true fan. Authors like Jim Cotton (61 articles), Andrew Hood (53 articles), and Shane Stokes (36 articles) became familiar voices guiding you through race coverage. You didn't just skim headlines\u2014you dove deep into the sport's complexities, reading pieces about Visma-Lease a Bike's winter training secrets, exploring the careers of emerging talents like Ben Healy and Jonathan Milan, and staying informed on everything from UCI regulations to team controversies.\n\nBut cycling wasn't your only story. Throughout the year, you maintained a strong connection to trail-based adventure, regularly checking Trailforks (3,452 total events across the app and website) and using mapping tools to explore routes and trail conditions. You engaged with outdoor adventure content around national parks and public lands, showing particular interest in Yosemite (45 tagged articles). Your reading habits also revealed a curious, scientifically-minded explorer\u2014you read pieces on microplastics in the environment, sex differences in sport, and vitamin D supplementation, suggesting you approach outdoor pursuits with both passion and informed skepticism.\n\nAs summer faded into fall, your engagement remained strong through September (3,790 events) before gradually settling into a more moderate pace through the winter months. The rhythm of your year mirrors the cycling calendar itself\u2014ramping up for the grand tours, staying engaged through the season, then finding new rhythms as the year turned. You've been a model of consistent, thoughtful engagement across our entire ecosystem.\n\n---\n\n## Your Passions\n\n- **Professional Road Cycling** \u2014 Your most dominant passion by far. You consumed 200+ Tour de France articles, followed individual riders obsessively (Poga\u010dar, Vingegaard, Evenepoel), and stayed current on team dynamics, training methods, and racing drama. VeloNews became your second home with 3,843 events.\n\n- **Mountain Biking & Trail Culture** \u2014 An active participant in the Trailforks community (3,452 total events), you regularly check trail conditions, explore routes, and engage with the mountain biking community. This isn't passive reading\u2014you're actively logging activities and following fellow riders.\n\n- **Public Lands & National Parks Adventure** \u2014 You consistently engaged with content about Yosemite, national parks, and public lands conservation (65+ tagged articles). You're invested in where you can explore and how these spaces are protected.\n\n- **Outdoor Science & Health** \u2014 Beyond the romance of adventure, you dig into the data. You read about microplastics in the environment, sex differences in athletic performance, nutrition science, and wellness trends. You're an informed adventurer who wants to understand the \"why\" behind outdoor pursuits.\n\n- **Gear & Design** \u2014 With 47 tagged articles on design and tech, plus specific interest in footwear and sandals (43 articles each), you care about the tools that enable your adventures. You're interested in both the practical and innovative sides of outdoor equipment.\n\n- **Relationships & Adventure Culture** \u2014 You read Outside's culture section thoughtfully, including pieces on relationships, advice columns, and the human stories behind adventure. This suggests you see outdoor pursuits as deeply connected to how we live and relate to each other.\n\n---\n\n## Reading Habits\n\nYour reading patterns reveal someone who integrates Outside content into the rhythms of daily life. You're most active during evening hours (6 PM to midnight accounts for your heaviest engagement, with peaks at 8 PM and 9 PM), suggesting you settle in with our content after work or outdoor activities. You're also a night owl\u2014between 11 PM and 4 AM, you maintain consistent engagement, indicating you often read late into the evening or early morning hours. This is someone who makes time for deep dives into stories, not just quick scrolls.\n\nWeekdays dominate your reading calendar, with Monday through Wednesday showing the strongest engagement (3,500+ events each). This pattern suggests you use our content as part of your weekday routine\u2014perhaps reading during commutes, lunch breaks, or evening wind-downs\u2014while your engagement naturally drops on weekends (1,750 events on Saturday, 2,252 on Sunday). You're clearly balancing reading about adventure with actually doing it.\n\nYour favorite authors tell their own story. Jim Cotton and Andrew Hood, both cycling journalists, received your most consistent attention, followed by Shane Stokes. These aren't household names to casual readers, but they are to serious cycling fans\u2014they're the voices providing depth, analysis, and insider perspective on professional racing. Your reading choices reflect a preference for specialist expertise over generalist coverage. You've encountered 97 unique authors and 959 unique articles across 200 different tags, demonstrating genuine curiosity and breadth, but your core engagement centers on a tight group of trusted voices covering the sports and topics you care about most.",
